---
import Explorer from "../components/Explorer";
import { allSources } from "../data/cosmos";
import "../styles/global.css";

const sources = allSources();
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en-AU">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Zoom out from the Sun to the edge of the observable universe. Every step outward trades detail for context."
    />
    <title>The Sun, in Context</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="site-header">
      <nav aria-label="Primary">
        <a href="#top">The Sun, in Context</a>
        <a href="#journey-text">Text version</a>
        <a href="#sources">Sources</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main id="top">
      <div class="intro">
        <h1>The Sun, in Context</h1>
        <p class="intro__standfirst">
          Zoom out until home disappears. The Sun only looks like the centre of
          everything because you haven&rsquo;t pulled back yet &mdash; and every
          step outward trades detail for the context that was missing.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* client:load, never client:only: the invariants parse the built HTML,
          so the labels, the readout and the text journey all have to exist on
          disk before a single line of JavaScript runs. */}
      <Explorer client:load />

      <section class="sources" id="sources" data-testid="sources" aria-label="Sources">
        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <p>
          Every distance and size on this page was read off one of these pages
          during development, not recalled from memory. Each object&rsquo;s own
          details panel cites the specific page its figure came from, and the
          date it was read.
        </p>
        <ul>
          {
            sources.map((source) => (
              <li>
                <a href={source.url}>{source.name}</a>
                <span class="sources__accessed"> — read {source.accessed}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="ending" aria-label="In closing">
        <p class="ending__line">
          At the start, the Sun filled the screen. Its entire neighbourhood is
          now smaller than the marker standing in for it.
        </p>
        <p class="ending__line">
          Pulling back did not make home matter less. It showed how much context
          was missing from the first view.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <p>
        A student prototype for COMP4020 Agentic Coding Studio. Data from NASA,
        NASA/JPL and published astronomy literature, cited above. Interaction
        informed by TheSkyLive&rsquo;s 3D Solar System Viewer (camera anchoring,
        orbit lines as spatial context) and NASA&rsquo;s Eyes on the Solar
        System (continuous camera scaling, semantic labels, object focus). The
        implementation, artwork and data model here are original; no assets,
        code or branding were taken from either.
      </p>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
